# Trivy ignore file for the turma-agent + turma image scans
# (.github/workflows/turma-agent-image-scan.yml, turma-image-scan.yml).
#
# Only REVIEWED, non-actionable findings belong here, each with a reason. Any
# HIGH/CRITICAL not listed here still fails the scan — this is a triage list,
# not a mute button. Revisit entries when the upstream fix reaches our base image.

# CVE-2026-39822 — Go stdlib os.Root symlink-following / directory-traversal.
# Present only in bundled third-party Go binaries (docker CLI 29.6.1, gh 2.96.0
# and terraform 1.15.8), compiled with Go 1.26.4 / 1.25.10; the fix is Go
# 1.25.12 / 1.26.5. All three are already the newest upstream releases (docker
# 29.6.1 2026-06-26, gh 2.96.0 2026-07-02, terraform 1.15.8), so
# DOCKER_CLI_VERSION / GH_VERSION / TERRAFORM_VERSION can't be bumped yet —
# they'll pick up the patched toolchain on their next rebuild. Not first-party
# code and not reachable via attacker-controlled symlinks in this container. The
# expiry forces a revisit: bump the pins once upstream ships Go-1.26.5+ rebuilds.
CVE-2026-39822 exp:2026-10-10

# CVE-2026-27145 / CVE-2026-42504 — Go stdlib DoS: unbounded work parsing a
# crafted x509 DNS name / MIME header. Both reach us only through terraform
# 1.15.8's own Go 1.25.10 build (fix: Go 1.25.11+), and 1.15.8 IS the latest
# terraform, so TERRAFORM_VERSION has nothing to bump to — the next HashiCorp
# release built on a patched toolchain clears both. Third-party, and a DoS
# against a CLI that a session runs by hand against certs and metadata it
# already chose to fetch: the blast radius is that one terraform invocation.
# Expiry forces the revisit.
CVE-2026-27145 exp:2026-10-10
CVE-2026-42504 exp:2026-10-10

# GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf — HIGH gRPC-Go xDS RBAC / HTTP/2 vulnerability (fixed in
# grpc-go 1.82.1). Embedded in TWO bundled third-party Go binaries — gh 2.96.0
# (grpc v1.81.1) and terraform 1.15.8 (grpc v1.80.0) — not first-party code and
# not independently patchable, since grpc is compiled into each binary. Both are
# already the newest upstream releases (see the Go-toolchain entries above), so
# GH_VERSION / TERRAFORM_VERSION have nothing to bump to; the next upstream
# release built against grpc 1.82.1+ clears it. The vulnerable path is a gRPC
# xDS/RBAC server, which neither the gh nor terraform CLI runs here. Expiry
# forces the revisit.
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf exp:2026-10-10

# GHSA-537c-gmf6-5ccf — the cryptography wheel bundles a vulnerable OpenSSL.
# From azure-cli's OWN vendored python (/opt/az, cryptography 46.0.7; fixed in
# 48.0.1), not anything this repo installs — az ships and pins that tree itself,
# so the only lever here is AZURE_CLI_VERSION, and 2.88.0 is already the newest
# package Microsoft publishes for bookworm. Revisit on the expiry: a later
# azure-cli vendoring cryptography 48+ clears it with a pin bump.
GHSA-537c-gmf6-5ccf exp:2026-10-10

# CVE-2026-47302 / -47304 / -50525 / -50527 / -50648 — HIGH .NET
# (System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 10.0.6) DoS / security-feature-bypass CVEs,
# all fixed in dotnet 10.0.10 / 9.0.18 / 8.0.4. From azure-cli's OWN vendored
# .NET runtime, not anything this repo installs — az bundles and pins that tree
# itself, so the only lever is AZURE_CLI_VERSION, and 2.88.0 is already the
# newest package Microsoft publishes for bookworm (same story as GHSA-537c
# above). Not first-party code; the blast radius is the `az` CLI a session runs
# by hand. Revisit on the expiry: a later azure-cli bundling patched .NET clears
# them with a pin bump.
CVE-2026-47302 exp:2026-10-10
CVE-2026-47304 exp:2026-10-10
CVE-2026-50525 exp:2026-10-10
CVE-2026-50527 exp:2026-10-10
CVE-2026-50648 exp:2026-10-10

# CVE-2026-56852 — HIGH infinite loop in golang.org/x/text's norm.Iter on
# crafted input (fixed in x/text 0.39.0). Present only in bundled third-party
# Go binaries — gh 2.96.0 (x/text 0.38.0) and terraform 1.15.8 (0.37.0) — where
# x/text is compiled in, not independently patchable. Both are already the
# newest upstream releases (gh 2.96.0 2026-07-02, terraform 1.15.8 latest
# stable), so GH_VERSION / TERRAFORM_VERSION have nothing to bump to; the next
# upstream release built against x/text 0.39+ clears it. Not first-party code,
# and a DoS against a CLI a session runs by hand on input it chose. The expiry
# forces the revisit: bump the pins once upstream ships x/text-0.39+ rebuilds.
CVE-2026-56852 exp:2026-10-10

# CVE-2026-46602 / CVE-2026-46604 — HIGH DoS in golang.org/x/image's TIFF
# decoder: unbounded allocation / panic on a crafted image (fixed in x/image
# 0.43.0). Present only in the bundled glab CLI (GLAB_VERSION 1.111.0), where
# x/image v0.20.0 is compiled in as an INDIRECT dep — not independently
# patchable. 1.111.0 is the newest upstream release (2026-07-30) and even
# glab's main branch still pins x/image v0.20.0, so GLAB_VERSION has nothing to
# bump to. Not first-party code, and a DoS against a CLI a session runs by hand
# — glab never decodes attacker-supplied TIFFs here. The expiry forces the
# revisit: bump GLAB_VERSION once upstream ships an x/image-0.43+ rebuild.
CVE-2026-46602 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-46604 exp:2026-10-25

# CVE-2026-14257 — HIGH ReDoS/DoS in brace-expansion through 5.0.7 (fixed in
# 5.0.8). Present ONLY in the npm bundled inside the node base image
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion), a transitive
# dep of npm itself. NPM_VERSION already pins npm ahead of the base image to
# clear such findings, but even the latest npm (12.0.1) still vendors
# brace-expansion 5.0.7 — there is nothing to bump to yet. Not first-party: the
# hub runs zero npm deps (node-core only) and never invokes npm at runtime, so
# the vulnerable regex is unreachable in this image. Revisit on the expiry: the
# first npm release vendoring brace-expansion 5.0.8+ clears it with an
# NPM_VERSION bump.
CVE-2026-14257 exp:2026-10-25

# CVE-2026-69152 — HIGH DoS in brace-expansion through 5.0.7 (unbounded
# intermediate arrays; fixed in 5.0.9). Same vendored copy as CVE-2026-14257
# above (npm's own node_modules inside the node base image), same story:
# checked 2026-08-04, the newest npm (12.0.2) still vendors brace-expansion
# 5.0.7, so NPM_VERSION has nothing to bump to and the regex is unreachable
# here (npm never runs at runtime). Revisit on the expiry: the first npm
# vendoring 5.0.9+ clears it with an NPM_VERSION bump.
CVE-2026-69152 exp:2026-10-25

# CVE-2026-69192 — HIGH in ip-address 10.2.0 (fixed in 10.3.1), a transitive
# dep (via socks) of the npm bundled inside the node base image
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/ip-address). Checked
# 2026-08-04: the newest npm (12.0.2) still vendors ip-address 10.2.0, so
# NPM_VERSION has nothing to bump to. Not first-party and unreachable at
# runtime — npm's SOCKS-proxy path never runs in this image. Revisit on the
# expiry: the first npm vendoring 10.3.1+ clears it with an NPM_VERSION bump.
CVE-2026-69192 exp:2026-10-25

# CVE-2026-69247 / CVE-2026-69249 — HIGH CVEs in python cryptography 46.0.7
# (fixed in 50.0.0 / 49.0.0). From azure-cli's OWN vendored python tree
# (/opt/az, same as GHSA-537c-gmf6-5ccf above), not anything this repo
# installs. Checked 2026-08-04: the newest bookworm package (2.89.0) still
# vendors cryptography 46.0.7, so AZURE_CLI_VERSION has nothing to bump to.
# Not first-party; the blast radius is the `az` CLI a session runs by hand.
# Revisit on the expiry: an azure-cli vendoring cryptography 49+/50+ clears
# both with a pin bump.
CVE-2026-69247 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-69249 exp:2026-10-25

# CVE-2026-46600 — HIGH DoS in golang.org/x/net's dns/dnsmessage parser:
# invalid DNS record parsing (fixed in x/net 0.56.0). The scan reports exactly
# one carrier, /usr/local/bin/terraform (TERRAFORM_VERSION 1.15.8, x/net
# v0.55.0), where x/net is compiled in and not independently patchable. Checked
# 2026-08-12 against HashiCorp's release index: 1.15.8 (2026-07-08) is still the
# newest STABLE terraform — everything after it is 1.16.0-rc/alpha — so
# TERRAFORM_VERSION has nothing to bump to, the same story as the terraform
# entries above. Not first-party, and a DoS against a CLI a session runs by
# hand: nothing here parses DNS responses from a resolver the operator didn't
# already choose. The expiry forces the revisit: bump TERRAFORM_VERSION once
# HashiCorp ships a stable release built against x/net 0.56+.
CVE-2026-46600 exp:2026-10-25

# CVE-2026-33818 / CVE-2026-39821 / CVE-2026-56853 / CVE-2026-56858 /
# CVE-2026-56859 / CVE-2026-56860 / CVE-2026-56862 — a batch of Go stdlib
# HIGHs published 2026-08 (asn1 Unmarshal recursion DoS, x/net/idna Punycode
# privilege escalation, unencrypted-HTTP/2 DoS, html/template XSS, encoding/xml
# recursion DoS, net/url quadratic path parsing, crypto/tls KeyUpdate DoS).
#
# Carriers are the four bundled third-party Go binaries, none first-party:
#   docker CLI 29.6.2  (Go 1.26.5)
#   gh 2.96.0          (Go 1.26.4)
#   glab 1.111.0       (Go 1.26.5)
#   terraform 1.15.8   (Go 1.25.10)
# The fixes are Go 1.25.13 / 1.26.6 / 1.27.0-rc.3.
#
# Checked 2026-08-18, and a version bump does NOT clear these — the newest
# upstream releases are still built on an unpatched toolchain. Verified by
# downloading each and reading its embedded Go version, not by assuming:
#   gh 2.97.0 -> go1.26.5, docker 29.7.2 -> go1.26.5, glab 1.113.0 -> go1.26.5.
# terraform 1.15.8 IS the newest stable, as the entries above already record.
# So every pin either has nothing to move to or moves to an equally-affected
# build; they clear on the next upstream rebuild against a patched Go.
#
# Not first-party code, and the reachable surface is a CLI a session runs by
# hand against endpoints and files the operator already chose. The expiry forces
# the revisit rather than letting the batch sit: by then Go 1.26.6 rebuilds of
# all four should exist and the pins can move.
CVE-2026-33818 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-39821 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-56853 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-56858 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-56859 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-56860 exp:2026-10-25
CVE-2026-56862 exp:2026-10-25
